-- Migration: 0015_outline_and_assets
--
-- Two columns, both written by the ingest from the SAME page walk that already
-- finds section headings, and both answering a client request that until now had
-- no data behind it.
--
-- ── 1. standards.outline_json — DO082 ────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- "Provide a 'View Table of Contents' option for each standard on the 'List
--  Standards' page. This could either be generated by indexing or could be
--  pulled from the 'preview' files … Goal: provide easy access to TOC for each
--  document" — and, explicitly, "even if the user is on LensyLite
--  (non-subscriber)".
--
-- Generated by indexing, because that needs nothing new hosted and nothing new
-- licensed: the headings are already detected for the section titles (migration
-- 0011). What 0011's map cannot carry is ORDER and PAGE, which is exactly what a
-- table of contents is, so this column holds the array:
--
--   [{ "number": "8.2", "title": "Community Lighting Plan", "page": 54 }, …]
--
-- in document order. src/lib/chunker.js extractOutline() produces it, and
-- extractSectionTitles() is now derived from it, so the MAP and the OUTLINE can
-- never disagree. (The chunker's own walk is still separate — it must see every
-- line, including the reference and table-of-contents pages the outline skips —
-- so a chunk's `section` and the outline can still differ on a document whose
-- numbering the font mangled. That is the same fail-open case DO071 describes:
-- the card prints a bare number rather than a wrong title.)
--
-- Served by GET /api/standards/:id/outline, which is gated on a session but NOT
-- on a corpus tier: a table of contents is metadata about a document, not the
-- document, and the client asked for it to be free.
--
-- ── 2. standards.assets_json — DO086 ─────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- "It doesn't seem to 'understand' tables and images as well as basic text …
--  Allow users to find images, tables, and the content within them via effective
--  search card results."
--
-- The embedding model is text-only, and a table or figure that is a raster image
-- yields no text at all — so its CELLS cannot be searched without OCR or a
-- vision model (see CLAUDE.md for what that would take). Its CAPTION, however, is
-- always text, always names the thing, and always has a page:
--
--   [{ "kind": "table", "label": "Table C-1",
--      "caption": "Sound Absorption Coefficients for Various Materials",
--      "page": 62 }, …]
--
-- With this, "where is the table that shows sound coefficients for various
-- materials?" answers with the table's number and page even though its contents
-- are an image — the client's own failing example.
--
-- Both columns are optional: a standard ingested before this migration simply
-- has no outline (the Table of Contents falls back to the section-title map,
-- without pages) and no asset chips.

ALTER TABLE standards ADD COLUMN outline_json TEXT;
ALTER TABLE standards ADD COLUMN assets_json TEXT;
